Here's how to wire it up:

```csharp
// MauiProgram.cs
public static class MauiProgram
{
    public static MauiApp CreateMauiApp()
    {
        var builder = MauiApp.CreateBuilder();
        builder.UseMauiApp<App>();

        // HttpClient + data service: one instance shared app-wide
        builder.Services.AddSingleton<HttpClient>();
        builder.Services.AddSingleton<IDataService, DataService>();

        // ViewModels: new instance per request (each navigation gets fresh state)
        builder.Services.AddTransient<MainViewModel>();
        builder.Services.AddTransient<DetailViewModel>();

        // Pages: transient so each navigation creates a new page
        builder.Services.AddTransient<MainPage>();
        builder.Services.AddTransient<DetailPage>();

        return builder.Build();
    }
}
```

**Why these lifetimes:**

| Registration | Lifetime | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `HttpClient` | Singleton | Reusing one instance avoids socket exhaustion; thread-safe by design. |
| `IDataService` | Singleton | Holds the shared `HttpClient`, caches, or auth tokens — no per-page state. |
| `MainViewModel` | Transient | Each page navigation should start with a clean VM (no stale data from a previous visit). |
| `DetailViewModel` | Transient | Same reason — the detail item changes each time. |
| `MainPage` / `DetailPage` | Transient | MAUI Shell navigation expects fresh page instances; avoids visual state leaking between navigations. |

**Rule of thumb:**
- **Singleton** → stateless shared infrastructure (HTTP clients, services, caches).
- **Transient** → anything tied to UI navigation (pages, view models).
- **Scoped** → rarely used in MAUI (no built-in request scope like ASP.NET), but useful if you create your own scope per navigation stack.

> **Tip:** If you later need a typed HTTP client with resilience policies, swap `AddSingleton<HttpClient>()` for `builder.Services.AddHttpClient<IDataService, DataService>()` from `Microsoft.Extensions.Http` — it manages handler lifetimes for you.
